00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
dna is perfect for the storage and  readout of genetic information  this information is stored in the  sequence of adenine thymine  guanine and cytosine also called a t g  and c  each helix has a backbone made of  alternating deoxyribose and phosphate  groups  readout occurs in three prime to five  prime direction making use of the  pairing of bases between the opposing  strands of the dna double helix in these  pairings a pairs with t and g pairs with  c via sets of complementary hydrogen  bonds  the edges of the base pairs are exposed  in the two grooves of the double helix  the wider major groove and the narrower  minor groove  these edges are also used to carry  information that is read by proteins  that interact with the double helix  prime example of a dna double helix is  pdb entry 1bna  upon printing the dna folding template  you will notice that it contains two  types of models  in this model you can add your own base  pairs but be sure to pair them up  properly  this detailed model shows all the atoms  in each nucleotide  in this tutorial we will use the smaller  model for the demonstration however the  technique is the same for both models  first cut out the model  fold all long solid gray lines so that  each solid line is visible on the top  edge of the crease that is made  fold all long dotted gray lines so that  each long dotted line is hidden within  the crease that is made  fold the paper in half so that the outer  two backbone columns stack one over the  other  tuck the other backbone flaps with five  prime at the top one over the other  next  temporarily fold the backbones so that  the model is flat  crease the horizontal and diagonal lines  solid lines should be visible on the top  edge of each crease with dotted lines on  the inside  you can crease them in any order you  like folding and unfolding each intern  one good way to do it is to crease all  of the horizontal lines then flip the  model over and crease all of the  diagonal lines  the result should look sort of like a  paper fan  using the creases that you just made  fold out triangles that stack on top of  each other but not directly  this way the triangle beneath the one  just folded should rotate a little bit  further clockwise and as this process  continues the triangles in the spiraling  ladder will form the circle structure  your model should look like this when  all the lines have been folded  pull the model open and pop out the  backbones on the sides  your finished model is a right-handed  double helix  you can use the tabs to connect several  models to make longer strands  to learn more about dna go to pdb 101.
 

